// **the one argument position a literal still cannot take.** everywhere else the compiler hoists a
// declaration ahead of the statement and puts its name in the literal's place - but a hoist runs
// unconditionally, and everything under a `?->` runs only when the base was there. so the elements
// would be evaluated on the null path too, which is an effect the author never wrote
//
// `?->` and `??` are the only two forms in the language that do not evaluate their right side, so this
// is a two-arm rule rather than a control-flow analysis. bind the literal to a name and the refusal
// goes away, which is what the message says

class Holder
{
    int32 $n;

    constructor(int32 $n) { $this->n = $n; }

    function take(Array<int32>& $a) : int32 { return $a[0]; }
}

Holder? $h = null;

echo $h?->take([1, 2]) ?? 7;
